//? Title: von Mises fitting
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Fit the circular analogue of the normal distribution by maximum likelihood, correct the small-sample bias in its concentration, and recognise when the data cannot identify a mean direction at all

The von Mises distribution \(f(\theta)=e^{\kappa\cos(\theta-\mu)}/(2\pi I_0(\kappa))\) is the standard unimodal model for directions. \(\mu\) is the mean direction and \(\kappa\ge0\) the concentration. \(\kappa=0\) is the uniform distribution, and large \(\kappa\) gives an approximately normal distribution with variance \(1/\kappa\). The maximum-likelihood estimates have closed forms in terms of the sample:

- The mean direction is \(\hat\mu=\operatorname{atan2}(\bar S,\bar C)\).
- The concentration solves \(A_1(\hat\kappa)=\bar R\), where \(A_1=I_1/I_0\) is the von Mises mean resultant length.

Two features make the fit delicate. First, \(\hat\kappa\) is biased upward in small samples, because \(\bar R\) is biased upward: even uniform data have \(E\bar R\approx\sqrt{\pi/(4n)}\). Second, as \(\bar R\to0\) the likelihood for \(\mu\) becomes flat. The estimate \(\hat\mu\) is still computed, but it is then determined by noise.

`von_mises_fit` returns:

- \(\hat\mu\) and \(\hat\kappa\);
- the information-matrix standard errors of `circular::mle.vonmises`;
- a Wald interval for \(\mu\);
- a profile-likelihood interval for \(\kappa\), which reaches 0 when uniformity is plausible;
- the log-likelihood and AIC;
- three diagnostics: `boundary` (\(\hat\kappa=0\)), the Rayleigh p-value, and `vanishing_resultant` (no evidence of a mean direction).

By default it solves \(A_1(\kappa)=\bar R\) exactly through Bessel-ratio recurrences. Alternatively it can use Fisher's piecewise approximation, as `circular` does, and it offers the Best–Fisher bias correction. `von_mises_a1`, `von_mises_a1_inverse`, `von_mises_log_likelihood` and `von_mises_cdf` are exposed as well. The CDF comes from the density's Fourier series and can serve as Kuiper's null for goodness of fit. The tests match `mle.vonmises` (with and without `bias = TRUE`, and with a known \(\mu\)), an exact `uniroot` inverse and an R profile interval to about \(10^{-10}\), and `pvonmises` to \(10^{-10}\).

The study simulates 2000 samples per setting. It compares the three estimators of \(\kappa=2\) as \(n\) grows, along with the coverage of the 95% intervals, and then examines the diagnostics as \(\kappa\) approaches 0 with \(n=20\).

```{cpp, id=vmfit, file="figures/von_mises_fitting.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/circular.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
const double pi = std::numbers::pi;

double median(std::vector<double> v) {
    std::ranges::sort(v);
    return v[v.size() / 2];
}

int main() {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(4);
    const double kappa = 2.0, mu = 1.0;
    const int reps = 2000;
    std::println("| n | Mean kappa-hat: exact MLE | Fisher approximation | "
                 "Bias-corrected | Median MLE | Profile interval for kappa | "
                 "Wald interval for mu |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::vector<double> ns, mle_mean, cor_mean, med;
    for (const std::size_t n : {8, 10, 15, 20, 30, 50, 100}) {
        std::vector<double> ex, ap, co;
        double kc = 0, mc = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            const auto x = dm::stats::von_mises_sample(n, mu, kappa, rng.next_u64());
            const auto f = dm::stats::von_mises_fit(x);
            ex.push_back(std::min(f.concentration, 1e3));
            ap.push_back(std::min(dm::stats::von_mises_fit(x, {.exact_inverse = false})
                                      .concentration, 1e3));
            co.push_back(
                dm::stats::von_mises_fit(x, {.bias_correction = true}).concentration);
            kc += f.concentration_lower <= kappa && kappa <= f.concentration_upper;
            mc += !std::isnan(f.mean_lower) &&
                  std::abs(std::remainder(f.mean_direction - mu, 2 * pi)) <=
                      (f.mean_upper - f.mean_lower) / 2;
        }
        const auto mean = [](const std::vector<double>& v) {
            double s = 0;
            for (const double x : v)
                s += x / v.size();
            return s;
        };
        std::println("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", n,
                     mean(ex), mean(ap), mean(co), median(ex), kc / reps, mc / reps);
        ns.push_back(double(n));
        mle_mean.push_back(mean(ex)), cor_mean.push_back(mean(co));
        med.push_back(median(ex));
    }

    std::println("\n| kappa (n = 20) | Bias-corrected kappa-hat = 0 | "
                 "Vanishing-resultant flag | Mean kappa-hat | Wald interval for mu defined | Its coverage |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const double k : {0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}) {
        double b = 0, v = 0, km = 0, defined = 0, cover = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            const auto x = dm::stats::von_mises_sample(20, mu, k, rng.next_u64());
            const auto f = dm::stats::von_mises_fit(x);
            b += dm::stats::von_mises_fit(x, {.bias_correction = true}).boundary;
            v += f.vanishing_resultant, km += f.concentration / reps;
            if (!std::isnan(f.mean_lower)) {
                ++defined;
                cover += std::abs(std::remainder(f.mean_direction - mu, 2 * pi)) <=
                         (f.mean_upper - f.mean_lower) / 2;
            }
        }
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.2f} | {:.3f} | {} |", k, b / reps,
                     v / reps, km, defined / reps, k == 0 ? std::string("(no true mu)")
                                                : std::format("{:.3f}", cover / defined));
    }

    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(ns, mle_mean, "Mean of exact MLE", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .points(ns, mle_mean, "", {176, 72, 43}, 4)
        .line(ns, med, "Median of exact MLE", {225, 160, 140}, 2)
        .line(ns, cor_mean, "Mean of bias-corrected estimate", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .points(ns, cor_mean, "", {26, 90, 147}, 4)
        .abline_h(kappa, {120, 120, 120})
        .y_limits(1.5, 3.6)
        .title("Estimating kappa = 2: small-sample bias")
        .x_label("Sample size n")
        .y_label("Estimated concentration")
        .size(620, 400);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/von_mises_fitting.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| n | Mean kappa-hat: exact MLE | Fisher approximation | Bias-corrected | Median MLE | Profile interval for kappa | Wald interval for mu |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 2.89 | 2.88 | 2.03 | 2.33 | 0.913 | 0.911 |
| 10 | 2.60 | 2.59 | 1.99 | 2.25 | 0.933 | 0.916 |
| 15 | 2.32 | 2.32 | 1.97 | 2.16 | 0.940 | 0.923 |
| 20 | 2.26 | 2.25 | 2.01 | 2.14 | 0.951 | 0.942 |
| 30 | 2.15 | 2.14 | 2.00 | 2.07 | 0.951 | 0.936 |
| 50 | 2.09 | 2.08 | 2.00 | 2.06 | 0.954 | 0.932 |
| 100 | 2.05 | 2.04 | 2.01 | 2.02 | 0.948 | 0.946 |

