| 2 | 0.000 | 0.002 | 2.22 | 1.000 | 0.931 |
\image(figures/von_mises_fitting.svg)
// result_end

The exact MLE and Fisher's approximation agree to within 0.01 everywhere, so the approximation in `circular` is harmless for point estimation. Both are biased upward: on average \(\hat\kappa\) is 2.89 at \(n=8\) and 2.26 at \(n=20\) for a true \(\kappa=2\). The bias falls roughly as \(1/n\), and the skewed sampling distribution puts the median below the mean. The Best–Fisher correction removes the bias from \(n=8\) onward. The profile-likelihood interval for \(\kappa\) reaches 95% coverage by \(n=15\). The Wald interval for \(\mu\) runs 1–3 points below nominal until about \(n=100\), because it plugs in the upward-biased \(\hat\kappa\) and so is too narrow.

Near uniformity the fit is misleading. With \(\mu\) estimated, \(\bar R\) is always positive, so the uncorrected \(\hat\kappa\) is never 0. Uniform data of size 20 give an average \(\hat\kappa\) of 0.41, and a finite Wald interval for a non-existent mean direction in 84% of samples. The vanishing-resultant flag (Rayleigh \(p>0.05\)) catches 95% of these samples. At \(\kappa=0.25\) the flag is still raised 90% of the time, and the Wald intervals that do exist cover only 76%: the reported direction is noise around \(\mu\), and the interval does not know it. The bias-corrected estimate reaches the boundary \(\hat\kappa=0\) in 37% of uniform samples, a second, more conservative signal. Only from \(\kappa\approx1\) (where \(n\bar R^2\approx4.6\)) does the flag become rare and the interval approach nominal coverage.

>> Practical guidance

- Report \(\hat\mu\), \(\hat\kappa\) and the profile interval for \(\kappa\). An interval reaching 0 means uniformity is consistent with the data, and then \(\hat\mu\) should not be interpreted.
- Check the Rayleigh p-value, or the `vanishing_resultant` flag, before interpreting the mean direction. A computed \(\hat\mu\) with a finite interval is not evidence that a direction exists.
- For \(n<30\), report the bias-corrected \(\hat\kappa\), or at least note the upward bias. Dispersion-based comparisons between groups of different sizes are otherwise confounded by sample size.
- Check the von Mises shape with Kuiper's test against `von_mises_cdf` at the fitted parameters (the p-value is then conservative, so simulate from the fit when it is near the threshold). Use the sample summaries instead when the data are skewed, bimodal or heavy-tailed.

>> References

- N. I. Fisher (1993). *Statistical Analysis of Circular Data*. Cambridge University Press. doi:10.1017/CBO9780511564345
- D. J. Best and N. I. Fisher (1981). The bias of the maximum likelihood estimators of the von Mises-Fisher concentration parameters. *Communications in Statistics - Simulation and Computation* 10(5), 493-502. doi:10.1080/03610918108812225
- K. V. Mardia and P. E. Jupp (2000). *Directional Statistics*. Wiley. doi:10.1002/9780470316979
- A. Pewsey, M. Neuhäuser and G. D. Ruxton (2013). *Circular Statistics in R*. Oxford University Press.
